<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nefertari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1255 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Nefertari Meritmut, Naptera</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nefertari, also Nefertari Meritmut, was an Egyptian queen and the first of the Great Royal Wives of Ramesses the Great, whom she married before he became king. She bore at least four sons and two daughters, among them the crown prince Amun-her-khepeshef. Able to read and write hieroglyphs, a rare skill, she corresponded with other royals: tablets from the Hittite capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusas</a> preserve her letters, as Naptera, to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puduhepa">Puduhepa</a>. Ramesses built a temple for her at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> beside his own. She was buried in QV66, one of the largest tombs of the Valley of the Queens, robbed in antiquity and excavated by Ernesto Schiaparelli in 1904.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nefertari.jpg" alt="Nefertari" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Doudoroff · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Nefertari_(52785688763).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Queens such as Tiye and <b>Nefertari</b> had real influence, the God’s Wife of Amun was the highest priestess of Amun at Thebes, and Hatshepsut ruled as king.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
